eropoint subtraction bias","bic":384.11}],"verdict":{"hash":"53c89112778d","time":"2019-06-19T22:12:25+00:00","verdict":"neptune","log10_e":37.826,"n":8}},{"hash":"10413af58871","time":"2019-06-19T22:12:25+00:00","chosen":"Galactic point-lens microlensing","proposals":[{"leap":"hidden","name":"Microlensed underlying supernova","bic":42.57},{"leap":"new","name":"Galactic point-lens microlensing","bic":32.02},{"leap":"instrument","name":"Drifting template subtraction leakage","bic":81.5}],"verdict":{"hash":"15360744cc94","time":"2019-06-30T22:12:25+00:00","verdict":"abstain","log10_e":0.264,"n":13}}],"said":["Many stars across the field show dipole/ring residuals in the difference, indicating a failed subtraction rather than a clean transient.","The r band rose steadily by about 0.7 mag over 10 days to about 18.7 mag with persistently red g-r near 1 mag, matching a dust-reddened Ia rising to maximum around day 0 at z near 0.08. The Ia template reproduces this rise with chi2 per point of 1.31 using a luminous broad slow decliner with strong reddening, consistent with an extinguished normal Ia.","Twelve detections across ten nights in both g and r brighten smoothly from r~19.4 to ~18.7 with no single-night spike, erratic jumps, or scatter around zero.","q0 has the highest bits at 0.085 and directly tests continued rise versus peak around day 0 to separate the leading dust-reddened Ia from Ibc while near-future data can still settle it.","It is about as likely as not that this light will look brighter three days from now than it did at its last check.",null,null,"At this low Galactic latitude an unseen foreground point lens is moving into alignment with the background source. Its increasing magnification boosts the g and r fluxes by the same time-dependent factor, explaining the accelerating excess over the leveling supernova forecasts.",null,"The light curve shows a smooth ~24 d rise from r~19.4 to peak r~16.2 with near-constant g-r~1.0 and a turnover in both bands, matching an achromatic microlensing brightening with chi2 per point 0.22 and weight 0.9. Its location at galactic latitude 5 deg with no host and slow symmetric shape favors an unseen foreground lens over an extragalactic explosion.","q0 has the highest bits at 0.18 and its confident No directly tests the adopted microlensing decline so a Yes would refute the leader","It is very unlikely that this fading object will appear brighter three days from now than it did at its last sighting.",null,null],"facts":{"reader":0.15,"skeptics":{"instrument":"real","physics":"doubts ii, ibc, tde","memory":"does not recognise it"},"theories":["ibc","ia","ii"],"surprise":{"misses":4,"n":8,"p_value":1.0500916095999999e-05},"proposals":["Unseen foreground microlens","Galactic microlensing event","Drifting zeropoint subtraction bias"],"chosen":"Unseen foreground microlens","leap_cost":0.19732,"verdict":{"verdict":"neptune","log10_e":37.826,"n":8},"second":{"verdict":"abstain","log10_e":0.264,"n":13},"answer":false,"cost":0.95,"calls":53}}